WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:07.535
OFX, QIF, CSV, CAMT and MT940: bank statement files explained.

2
00:00:09.035 --> 00:00:13.516
What each bank download format holds, which one to pick for a money app

3
00:00:13.516 --> 00:00:18.251
or a spreadsheet, and how to convert them without uploading your statement.

4
00:00:19.651 --> 00:00:25.069
Why the format matters. A bank statement is a list of rows: a date, an amount,

5
00:00:25.069 --> 00:00:30.488
some text about who you paid, and sometimes a reference and a running balance.

6
00:00:31.788 --> 00:00:36.631
The formats you will meet. OFX stands for Open Financial Exchange.

7
00:00:37.931 --> 00:00:40.077
What to do once it is converted.

8
00:00:40.077 --> 00:00:43.499
Converting is usually the first step, not the goal.

9
00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:47.009
Why I keep this on my own device.

10
00:00:47.009 --> 00:00:51.764
A statement shows where you live, where you shop, what you earn and who

11
00:00:51.764 --> 00:00:52.969
you send money to.

12
00:00:54.269 --> 00:00:58.069
To try it, open Bank Statement Converter in OpenFile.

13
00:00:58.069 --> 00:01:02.155
Drop in a file, adjust the settings and press the button.

14
00:01:02.155 --> 00:01:05.811
It all runs on your device, so nothing is uploaded.

15
00:01:07.511 --> 00:01:11.799
Read the full post, with live demos, on the OpenFile blog.
